# The Ledger — Data Credibility (lib/data · scripts · types)

Data credibility is the product within this policy's scope (`lib/data/**`, `scripts/**`, `lib/types/**`). Platform-wide binding rules: `.cursor/rules/ledger-core-rules.mdc`.

## Source tiers

**Canonical tier table:** `.cursor/rules/ledger-core-rules.mdc` §3 — use those exact code values in code and JSX; never write "Tier 1/2/3/4".

---

## Canonical definition of `'alleged'` (owner + Claude — 2026-07-26)

**`alleged` = a contested claim about a person** (accusation, unproven assertion, or individually
constructed opinion presented as fact). It is **not** a data-quality confidence score and must
never be used to paper over missing corroboration on official/primary records.

### Permitted surfaces (only two)

1. **Controversies**
2. **Claims inside a news item** — the article *listing* itself stays tier `'media'` for approved
   outlets (`isOpinion` when commentary). Contested claims nested in the article may be labeled
   `'alleged'`; failed corroboration of a news listing does **not** demote the listing to `'alleged'`.

### Banned surfaces (never assign `'alleged'`)

votes · Said/statements · Said→Did · positions/beliefs · finance · stock trades · lobbying/PAC ·
legislation · orgVoteLinks · endorsements.

These are official/primary (or endorsement) records. If content fails the evidentiary floor,
**OMIT** with an honest gap — never downgrade to `'alleged'`.

### Rules when `'alleged'` is used

1. **Verbatim-only** — direct quote + attribution + date + URL. No summarizing or paraphrase.
   The headline/title must **not** restate an allegation as fact. Cannot quote exactly → do not carry
   until a verifiable quote is obtained (**repair before removal** — see below).
2. **Multi-source transparency** — 2+ reliable outlets on the same unproven claim → display
   "reported by N outlets" and **keep** the `'alleged'` label. Reporter agreement never converts an
   accusation into fact — only a primary/official record does.
3. **Outcome required** — where the record shows a resolution (charges dropped, settled, convicted,
   retracted, investigation closed, campaign remedied, etc.), display it **with** the allegation.
   An allegation without its outcome is misleading.

### Repair before removal (binding — 2026-07-26 REJECT remedy)

An item that fails the verbatim or tier requirement is **RE-SOURCED or RE-QUOTED**, not deleted.
Deletion is only for items with **no verifiable source at all**. Thinning a profile by deleting
sourced facts is a defect. Verbatim-only exists to stop paraphrase drift — not to erase documented
public records that still have URLs and dates.

### Endorsements

Require the member's own statement **or** 2+ approved outlets; otherwise **OMIT** — never `'alleged'`.
When a documented public endorsement has a single approved-outlet citation, **re-tier to `'media'`**
(and note the single-outlet status) rather than deleting — repair before removal.

---

## Multi-source corroboration rule (non-negotiable)

Any statement attributed to a politician that comes from a `'media'` or lower tier source
**must be corroborated by at least 2 independent approved sources** before it can be
displayed as a **verified** Said/statement.

- **1 source, tier `'official'` or `'nonpartisan'`** → display as verified with that single source
- **1 source, tier `'media'` (Said/statement/quote)** → **OMIT** (honest gap) — never demote to `'alleged'`
- **2+ independent sources, tier `'media'`** → display as verified, list all sources
- **Cannot corroborate** → do not display the statement at all; show "No verified statement on record"

News **listings** from approved outlets keep listing tier `'media'`/`'nonpartisan'`/`'official'`;
use `isVerified` for corroboration status — do not rewrite listing tier to `'alleged'`.

A source corroborates another ONLY if it is independent (different outlet, different author,
different publication date). A wire service (AP/Reuters) and an outlet republishing that wire
are NOT independent — count as one source.

### Corroboration floor for unobtainable data (owner directive 2026-07-19)

When a required fact is **not obtainable from any `'official'`/`'nonpartisan'` source** in the
source constitution (`docs/OBJECTIVE_SOURCES.md`, rule 4 — the single definition of this floor):

- A **single** below-standard provider is **never shown** — uncorroborated below-standard
  sourcing is an honest gap (`"No verified record available"`), not a display.
- Only when **two or more independent** below-standard providers corroborate may a
  *non-person-accusation* fact be shown, visibly flagged, with every provider cited alongside its
  lean label. Contested claims **about a person** follow the `'alleged'` definition above (Controversies
  / nested news claims only) — never park them on banned surfaces.
- Currently-unobtainable items (Senate eFD trades, OpenSecrets API, SAM.gov
  entity details) are tracked in `docs/OBJECTIVE_SOURCES.md` + `PROGRESS.md` — do not re-attempt
  blindly; render the honest gap.

---

## Banned sources

- **Wikipedia** — never under any tier. User-editable, not authoritative.
- Social media posts (Twitter/X, Facebook, Instagram) — not verifiable as tier 'official' even from a politician's own account unless corroborated by an approved outlet.
- Anonymous sources — never, at any tier.
- Press releases from campaigns or PACs — never sole sourcing for banned surfaces; contested
  person-claims may appear only under Controversies / nested news claims per the `'alleged'`
  definition above (verbatim + outcome).
- Any source without a verifiable URL and publication date.

---

## Journalism quotes (tier 'media')

Verbatim quotes from journalism must be:
- Exact — word for word, in quotation marks, with speaker attribution
- Linked to the original article URL
- Dated to the article's publication date
- Corroborated by 2+ approved outlets before displaying as verified (see rule above)
- **Never paraphrased** or summarized as if they are the politician's words

Approved journalism sources (tier 'media'):
NYT, Washington Post, Wall Street Journal, Politico, The Hill, AP, Reuters, NPR,
PBS NewsHour, Roll Call, CQ, The Atlantic, Bloomberg, ProPublica, The Guardian,
Miami Herald, Tampa Bay Times, Sun Sentinel, Orlando Sentinel, Florida Phoenix, WUSF, WLRN.

